Example: cat tutorial/orders.csv | proj category sum[sales]
For more information, open README.MD.

Directives:
  case[0-1 args]        Enables case-sensitive matching of field names, function names, and string comparisons (case-insensitive by default)
  help                  Prints this help text
  usage                 Prints this help text
  csvonly[0-1 args]     Skips JSON/XML/log-format detection and parses the input strictly as CSV/TSV
  in[1 arg]             Reads input from a file instead of standard input
  inheader[0-1 args]    Whether the input CSV/TSV has a header row (default: true)
  outheader[0-1 args]   Whether to print a header row in the output (default: true)
  header[0-1 args]      Whether to print a header row in the output (default: true)
  sep[1 arg]            Sets the output field separator (default: tab); also accepts the named alias "tab"
  join[1-2 args]        Joins another file's rows, matched with a where[] equality condition; pass true as a second argument for an outer join
  joinheader[0-1 args]  Whether the joined file has a header row (default: true)
  pivot[2-3 args]       Spreads repeated values into separate output columns
  sync[1 arg]           Emits a new output row each time the given path repeats, for combining sibling elements that don't share a common repeating parent
  root[1 arg]           Selects the nth top-level node as the document root for path matching
  where[1 arg]          Filters output rows to those where the given predicate is true
  first[1 arg]          Limits to the first n input rows, before filtering and sorting
  top[1 arg]            Limits to the top n output rows, after filtering and sorting
  sort[1-N args]        Sorts output rows by one or more expressions; prefix an expression with - for descending
  distinct[0-1 args]    Removes duplicate output rows
  hidden[1 arg]         Evaluates an expression as a named intermediate value, without adding an output column for it
Aggregate operators:
  any[1 arg]    First non-empty value encountered in the group
  sum[1 arg]    Sum of a numeric expression across the group
  avg[1 arg]    Average (mean) of a numeric expression across the group
  min[1 arg]    Smallest value of an expression across the group
  max[1 arg]    Largest value of an expression across the group
  var[1 arg]    Sample variance of a numeric expression across the group
  cov[2 args]   Covariance of two numeric expressions across the group
  corr[2 args]  Correlation coefficient of two numeric expressions across the group
  stdev[1 arg]  Sample standard deviation of a numeric expression across the group
  count[1 arg]  Number of rows in the group
String operators:
  len[1 arg]        Length of a string
  left[2 args]      First n characters of a string
  right[2 args]     Last n characters of a string
  lower[1 arg]      Converts a string to lowercase
  upper[1 arg]      Converts a string to uppercase
  contains[2 args]  True if the first string contains the second
  find[2 args]      Index of the second string within the first, or -1 if not found
Math operators:
  abs[1 arg]       Absolute value
  floor[1 arg]     Rounds down to the nearest integer
  ceil[1 arg]      Rounds up to the nearest integer
  round[1-2 args]  Rounds to the given number of decimal places (0 by default)
  min[2 args]      Smaller of two values
  max[2 args]      Larger of two values
  sqrt[1 arg]      Square root
  pow[2 args]      First value raised to the power of the second
  log[1-2 args]    Logarithm, base e unless a second argument gives the base
  exp[1 arg]       e raised to the power of the value
Type conversion operators:
  type[1 arg]      Name of a value's type ("int", "real", "str", "bool", or "datetime")
  real[1 arg]      Converts to a real (floating-point) number
  int[1 arg]       Converts to an integer
  bool[1 arg]      Converts to a boolean
  str[1-2 args]    Converts to a string
  datetime[1 arg]  Converts to a datetime value
Structural operators:
  ..[2 args]          Infix form (element..attribute) that accesses an XML attribute's value
  linenum[1 arg]      Line number in the input where the matched node appears
  depth[1 arg]        Nesting depth of the matched node
  path[1 arg]         Fully-qualified path of the matched node
  pivotpath[1 arg]    Pivoted path (dot-separated ancestor names) of the matched node, for use inside pivot[]
  parent[1 arg]       Name of the immediate parent node (shorthand for nodename[path,1])
  nodename[1-2 args]  Name of the matched node, or an ancestor N levels up if a second argument is given
  nodenum[1-2 args]   Position of the matched node in document order, optionally relative to a named or leveled ancestor
  nodestart[1 arg]    Byte offset in the input where the matched node begins
  nodeend[1 arg]      Byte offset in the input where the matched node ends
Misc operators:
  rownum            The 1-based row number of the current output row
  if[3 args]        Returns the second argument if the first is true, otherwise the third
  formatsec[1 arg]  Formats a Unix timestamp in seconds as a readable date/time
  formatms[1 arg]   Formats a Unix timestamp in milliseconds as a readable date/time
  comma[1 arg]      Formats a number with thousands separators, e.g. comma[1234567] -> "1,234,567"
Unary and binary operators:
  not[1 arg]
  ![1 arg]
  -[1 arg]
  *[2 args]
  /[2 args]
  %[2 args]
  +[1-2 args]
  -[2 args]
  eq[2 args]
  ==[2 args]
  ne[2 args]
  !=[2 args]
  le[2 args]
  <=[2 args]
  ge[2 args]
  >=[2 args]
  lt[2 args]
  <[2 args]
  gt[2 args]
  >[2 args]
  and[2 args]
  &&[2 args]
  or[2 args]
  ||[2 args]
  xor[2 args]
  ^[2 args]
  &[2 args]
  concat[2 args]
